soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: one owner for the MIPS clock and resets

The standby park (0208) writes the MIPS clock 0x02001600 and resets
0x0200160c raw, and the restart driver that follows in this series
(sun50i-h713-mips) writes the same two registers. Two writers who do not
know of each other end up with different ideas of what state the core is
in. The reload plan's L2 asks for one owner: both raw, as U-Boot and the
test bench that passed on the HY310 do it.

The owner is the restart driver. It registers itself with
cpu_comm_set_mips_owner(), and from then on cpu_comm asks it two things:

 - whether there is a core to park. cpu_comm_mips_running() read bit 0 of
   the MIPS status 0x0306101c with no check. After a park the bus gate is
   off, and what that read does then (0, or a bus hang) is not known; the
   owner reads the block only with the gate on and the bus reset
   released. Bit 0 also reads 1 for a core held in its soft reset (V3 on
   the HY310), so the answer stays "a core is there", as before.
 - to park it. cpu_comm_mips_park() goes through the owner. The writes,
   the 12 ms between them, the read-back and the rollback on failure are
   those of the R1 park.

Without an owner, when the module is not loaded, cpu_comm parks raw as
before. A second owner gets -EBUSY. Taking the owner back waits for a
call into it. The park's error line now says the return code and whether
the calls are open again.

Compiled out of tree against the R1 kernel with W=1, no new warning. Not
measured.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
